.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt TERMINAL-SIZE 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm terminal-size
.Nd observe the current session terminal dimensions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl Fl watch
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints the observed rows and columns, in that order, separated by one space.
.Fl Fl watch
prints the initial size and every subsequent observed change, polling every
100 kernel ticks. Use Ctrl-C in the managed shell to stop it.
.Pp
SSH dimensions come from the client's PTY request and window-change messages.
They describe what that client reported, rather than a hardware measurement.
Without observed session dimensions the result is unknown.
There is no guessed 80 by 24 fallback.
.Pp
The query uses the session's read-only metadata Cell. It neither changes
terminal modes nor reads the shell's control channel.
A program can observe updates with the private native runtime terminal query;
C programs can use the private __absd_terminal_size helper.
These helpers do not implement ioctl, termios or Unix signals.
.Pp
TERM identifies the sanitized SSH client terminal type, when provided.
COLUMNS and LINES are snapshots refreshed at shell prompts and native
command startup; an already running command must query for live changes.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Unknown dimensions produce a diagnostic and status 1, including at startup
in watch mode. Invalid arguments produce usage and status 2.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr absd-sh 1
